[Expected Approach] Greedy with Net Balances - O(n^2) Time and O(n^2) SpaceThe final cash flow depends only on the net balance of every friend, which is the money the friend receives minus the money the friend pays. The original debts do not matter, since any payments that give every friend the same net balance are valid. We settle friends greedily, so that every payment goes from a friend who must pay to a friend who must receive.A friend with a negative balance must pay money, and a friend with a positive balance must receive money. All the balances add up to 0.Every friend who must receive money has to get at least his balance, so the total cash flow can never be less than the sum of all positive balances.If only the payers pay and only the receivers receive, the total cash flow is exactly this sum. So in every step, we settle the friend who must pay the most with the friend who must receive the most.The amount transferred is the smaller of the two balances, so at least one of them becomes 0 in every step.Working of the Approach:Create an array net[] of size n, where net[i] is the total money friend i receives minus the total money friend i pays.Create an n × n matrix result[][] filled with 0.Find the friend debtor with the smallest balance, and the friend creditor with the largest balance.If net[debtor] == 0, all balances are 0, so stop. The balances add up to 0, so the smallest balance is 0 only when everything is settled.Otherwise, compute amount = min(-net[debtor], net[creditor]), and set result[debtor][creditor] = amount.Add amount to net[debtor] and subtract it from net[creditor]. At least one of them becomes 0.Repeat from the third step, and finally return result[][].Illustration:Consider transaction[][] = [[0, 0, 50, 0], [0, 0, 0, 30], [0, 0, 0, 10], [0, 0, 0, 0]].Net balances: friend 0 pays 50, so -50. Friend 1 pays 30, so -30. Friend 2 receives 50 and pays 10, so +40. Friend 3 receives 30 and 10, so +40. So net[] = [-50, -30, 40, 40], and the total is 0.Round 1: the smallest balance is friend 0 (-50), and the largest is friend 2 (+40; friends 2 and 3 are tied, so the first one is taken). The amount is min(50, 40) = 40, so result[0][2] = 40. Now net[] = [-10, -30, 0, 40], and friend 2 is settled.Round 2: the smallest balance is friend 1 (-30), and the largest is friend 3 (+40). The amount is min(30, 40) = 30, so result[1][3] = 30. Now net[] = [-10, 0, 0, 10], and friend 1 is settled.Round 3: the smallest balance is friend 0 (-10), and the largest is friend 3 (+10). The amount is min(10, 10) = 10, so result[0][3] = 10. Now net[] = [0, 0, 0, 0], and both friends are settled.The smallest balance is now 0, so we stop.The settlement is [[0, 0, 40, 10], [0, 0, 0, 30], [0, 0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0, 0]], and the total cash flow is 40 + 30 + 10 = 80, which equals the sum of the positive balances (40 + 40).
